.context SCR2
 Simulation of a single-deck screen using the Karra procedure.

 This model simulates the performance of a vibrating screen of known
 dimensions and screen type. The model reports the loading conditions of
 the screen and assesses the suitability of the screen for the duty that
 it must perform in the flowsheet.

 Karra,V.K., Development of a model for predicting the screening
 performance of a vibrating screen.
 CIM Bulletin. No 804, April 1978, pp.167-171.                                     

 Water sprays can be used on the screen with this model.

 PARAMETERS:
  1..... Square mesh of aperture of the deck.
  2..... Wire diameter of the screen.
  3..... Angle of the screen with respsct to the horizontal.
  4..... Length of the screen.
  5..... Width of the screen.
  6..... Bulk density of the mineral (Kg/m^3)
  7..... Screen material type.
  8..... Surface water retained on the oversize.
  9..... Number of screens in parallel.


.context SCRN
 Simple ideal model for screening.
 The required area of the screen is calculated using the traditional
 screen capacity factors. The screen dimensions can be optionally
 specified to compare with the required area. This model can
 accommodate water sprays.

Parameters
  1....Mesh size of screen.
  2....Screen efficiency.
  3....Surface water on screen oversize.
Optional parameters
    4....Length of screen.
    5....Width of screen.
  6....Number of screens in parallel.

.context SCR1
 Model for wet screening proposed by RSC Rogers.
 REF: Powder technology 31(1982) 135-137
 This model has been found to be effective for wet screening and has
 been tested for coal slurries on a Derrick high frequency screen. The
 classification function is described by
       e = 1/(1 + exp(ALPHA(1-x^3))/x)
 with
       x = dp/d50c
 The short circuit to oversize follows the water split.  The actual
 classification is described by
       c = 1-A + A*e
 where A is the water split to undersize.

Parameters
 1... d50c size in meters.
 2... Water split to undersize A.
 3... Efficiency parameter ALPHA.

.context DSC1
 Simple ideal model for a double deck screen.
 The required area of the screen is calculated using the traditional
 screen capacity factors. The screen dimensions can be optionally
 specified to compare with the required area. This model can
 accommodate water sprays.

Parameters
 1....Mesh size of screen on the top deck.
 2....Screen efficiency for the top deck.
 3....Surface water on the top screen oversize.
 4....Mesh size of the screen on the lower deck.
 5....Screen efficiency for the lower deck.
 6....Surface water on the lower screen oversize.
 7....Screen dimensions (optional)
 8....Number of screens in parallel.

.context DWSC
 Dewatering screen using the model proposed by K L Ng. "Dewatering performance
 vibrating screens". Proc. Instn Mech Engrs.  Part E Journal of Process
 Mechanical Engineering.  204(1990)pp73-79.
 Undersize solid is carried through the screen in proportion to the water flow.

PARAMETERS:
 1....Ultimate moisture content of the material (%)
 2....Mesh size of the screen
 3....Length of the screen
 4....Width of the screen
 5....Angle of inclination (degrees)
 6....Vibration frequency (rpm)
 7....Amplitude of vibration
 8....Angle of vibration relative to screen surface (degrees)

.context THIC
 A simple model for the thickener.
 The model assumes that all solids leave in the underflow.

PARAMETERS
 1.........Percentage solids in the underflow.

.context FILT
 Simple model for the filter. All solids leave in the filter cake.

PARAMETERS
 1...Percentage of solids in the filter cake.

.context SPLT
 Model splits the feed equally into 2 or 3 streams.
 MODSIM determines the number of output streams from the flowsheet.

.context SPL1
 Model splits the feed into 2 or 3 unequal streams.
 MODSIM determines the number of output streams from the flowsheet.
 User must specify the fractional split.

PARAMETERS IN ORDER
 1....Number of output streams
 2....Fractional split to output stream 1
 3....Fractional split to output stream 2

.context NOP_
 This model passes the feed through the unit without change.

.context NOP
 This model passes the feed through the unit without change.

.context MIXR

 The mixr unit combines any number of streams and produces an appropriate
 outlet stream. If water enters the mixer unit you will be asked to specify
 the water flowrate to the unit or the percentage solids in the stream that
 leaves the mixer unit. If you specify the percentage solids, MODSIM will
 automatically adjust the water addition rate to suit the total solid feed
 rate to the mixer.

.context UMIL
  Model for a ball mill using three perfectly mixed regions in series.
  No classification between stages and post-classification is optional.
  This model allows the critical parameters for the Andrews-Mika diagram
  to be specified as parameters.
  PARAMETERS:
     1....Residence time in the first perfectly mixed region.
     2....Residence time in the second perfectly mixed region.
     3....Residence time in the third perfectly mixed region.
     4....Functional forms chosen for breakage function and specific
          breakage rate constant.
           1 = 3-parameter breakage function & 4-parameter rate constant
               without post classification.
           2 = 3-parameter breakage function & 4-parameter rate constant
               with post classification. Classification size = 3.99 mm
           4 = 4-parameter breakage function & 4-parameter rate constant
               with post classification. Classification size = 0.918 mm
     5....Choice of liberation model. 0 = None. 1 = Ljubljana. 2 = Beta function.


.context SEGB
 A model to describe the segregation in the surge bins at Rossing Uranium
 crusher plant. These surge bins feed two parallel conveyor belts and show
 significant size segregation because one apron feeder sees coarser material.
 The model gives the recovery as a function of size to the finer stream.

     PARAMETERS
     **********
 1.....Small size asymptote.
 2.....Recovery of intermediate sizes.
 3.....Critical size.
 4.....Slope of logarithmic portion.

.context WDMS
 A simple recovery model for a wet drum magnetic concentrator
        ALPHA = PARAM(2)*EXP(-PARAM(3)*SIZE(I)/SIZE(1))
        REC = (1.0 - ALPHA)*GRDV(J,1)**PARAM(1) + ALPHA
 Parameters:
      1...Exponent on the volumetric fraction non-magnetics to model
          the recovery.
      2...By-pass fraction to non magnetics.
      3...Exponential coefficient to reduce by-pass as size increases.

.context WDM2
 A simple model for a wet drum magnetic concentrator. The partition curve
 is Rosin-Rammler with the volumetric composition of the particle as the
 determining variable. The short circuit to non-magnetics increases
 exponentially as particle size decreases.
  ALPHA = PARAM(3)*EXP(-PARAM(4)*SIZE(I)/0.001)
  REC = 1.0 - EXP(-0.69315*(GRDV(J,1)/(1.0-PARAM(2)))**LAMBDA)
  REC = (1.0 - ALPHA)*REC + ALPHA
 Parameters:
      1...Sharpness Index.
      2...The grade of mineral 2 in the particle that has 50% recovery.
      3...Small size limit of short circuit fraction to non magnetics.
      4...Exponential coefficient to reduce by-pass as size increases.
      5...Water split to tail stream

.context WICY
 This model for a hydrocyclone with water injection at the apex.
 Based on model proposed by K. Udaya Bhaskar et al "Classification studies 
 of lead-zinc ore fines using water-injection cyclone". Int. J. Miner.
 Process. 77 (2005) pp80-94.
 Parameters:
      1....Apex diameter
      2....Vortex finder diameter
      3....Feed pressure
      4....Number of units in parallel

.context SJIG
 Model for a single stage jig based on the equilibrium
 stratification model.
 Reference:
 R P King, Modeling and Simulation of Mineral Processing Sytems. Society for Mining, Metallurgy, and Exploration, Inc., 
 Englewood, Colorado,2012, Section 7.3 
 Parameters:
 1...Relative height of the cutter in the bed.
 2...Specific stratification constant.

.context PAN1
 There is no model available for the pan.

.context PAN2
 There is no model available for the pan.

.context PAN3
 There is no model available for the pan.

.context PUMP
This model passes the feed straight through to simulate the operation of a pump.
Gland service water may be added direct to the unit.

.context WHIM
 This model is not yet available for the wet high-intensity magnetic
 separator.

.context JNES
 This model is not yet available for the Jones wet high-intensity magnetic
 separator.  The model is under development for CVRD Southern System iron ore
 mining operations
